\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SII}{\ignorespaces \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~B on the same 100 held-out synthetic scenarios (corrected model v2). Policy level: final outcome of each episode (TP reported and achieved, FP reported but not achieved, FN achieved but not reported, TN neither); the final state can differ between configurations because a rejection returns control to planning. Decision level: the verifier's first accept/withhold decision on the 74 scenarios in which the planner claims completion (46 truly complete), against the true state at that claim. Wilson 95\% intervals. Synthetic $p$-values and intervals are conditional on the simulation assumptions; they are not empirical tests of the agent. $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented (proposed).}}{4}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-baseline}{{SII}{4}{\textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~B on the same 100 held-out synthetic scenarios (corrected model v2). Policy level: final outcome of each episode (TP reported and achieved, FP reported but not achieved, FN achieved but not reported, TN neither); the final state can differ between configurations because a rejection returns control to planning. Decision level: the verifier's first accept/withhold decision on the 74 scenarios in which the planner claims completion (46 truly complete), against the true state at that claim. Wilson 95\% intervals. Synthetic $p$-values and intervals are conditional on the simulation assumptions; they are not empirical tests of the agent. $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented (proposed)}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SIII}{\ignorespaces \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Paired comparisons with $\Phi $ on the same synthetic scenarios (v2, post hoc). Discordant pairs $b/c$ ($b$: only the other configuration; $c$: only $\Phi $). FC: episode ends in a false completion (100 scenarios); $\Delta $FC: false completions per scenario of the other configuration minus $\Phi $, mean [2.5--97.5th percentiles] over 1000 regenerated sets with rates drawn from their Beta posteriors. Dec.: first decision correct (74 claims), exact McNemar $p$. Succ.: task achieved and reported (100 scenarios), exact McNemar $p$. MC acc.: mean decision accuracy over the regenerated sets. $^{\S  }$One cell is 0 by construction of the model ($\Phi $ only withholds claims that no verification accepts; enhanced validation withholds every claim $\Phi $ withholds), so no McNemar $p$ is given: it would only restate $b$ or $c$. $^{\P  }$Zero cell from the nesting of basic detection within $\Phi $ at the point-estimate rates; in the regenerated sets, where the two rates are drawn independently, $\Phi $-only false completions occur in 39\% of sets. Synthetic $p$-values and intervals are conditional on the simulation assumptions; they are not empirical tests of the agent.}}{4}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-tests}{{SIII}{4}{\textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Paired comparisons with $\Phi $ on the same synthetic scenarios (v2, post hoc). Discordant pairs $b/c$ ($b$: only the other configuration; $c$: only $\Phi $). FC: episode ends in a false completion (100 scenarios); $\Delta $FC: false completions per scenario of the other configuration minus $\Phi $, mean [2.5--97.5th percentiles] over 1000 regenerated sets with rates drawn from their Beta posteriors. Dec.: first decision correct (74 claims), exact McNemar $p$. Succ.: task achieved and reported (100 scenarios), exact McNemar $p$. MC acc.: mean decision accuracy over the regenerated sets. $^{\S }$One cell is 0 by construction of the model ($\Phi $ only withholds claims that no verification accepts; enhanced validation withholds every claim $\Phi $ withholds), so no McNemar $p$ is given: it would only restate $b$ or $c$. $^{\P }$Zero cell from the nesting of basic detection within $\Phi $ at the point-estimate rates; in the regenerated sets, where the two rates are drawn independently, $\Phi $-only false completions occur in 39\% of sets. Synthetic $p$-values and intervals are conditional on the simulation assumptions; they are not empirical tests of the agent}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {SIII-D}}Recovery (Study E; H4)}{4}{subsection.3.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SIV}{\ignorespaces \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~C: one component removed at a time, on the same 100 scenarios (policy level). Each row is a simulated configuration, not a run of the implementation; each corresponds to a switch in the code (\texttt  {verification\_mode}, \texttt  {enable\_memory}, \texttt  {enable\_recovery}). $^{\S  }$As in the code, disabling the recovery engine leaves the three inline retries of the verification node in place. Removal of stored evidence is not simulated: $\Phi $ reads the live page, so stored evidence affects the audit trail, not decisions (code behaviour). Succ.: paired task-success discordance $b/c$ with $\Phi $ and exact McNemar $p$. MC: means over 1000 regenerated sets. Synthetic $p$-values and intervals are conditional on the simulation assumptions; they are not empirical tests of the agent.}}{4}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-ablation}{{SIV}{4}{\textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~C: one component removed at a time, on the same 100 scenarios (policy level). Each row is a simulated configuration, not a run of the implementation; each corresponds to a switch in the code (\texttt {verification\_mode}, \texttt {enable\_memory}, \texttt {enable\_recovery}). $^{\S }$As in the code, disabling the recovery engine leaves the three inline retries of the verification node in place. Removal of stored evidence is not simulated: $\Phi $ reads the live page, so stored evidence affects the audit trail, not decisions (code behaviour). Succ.: paired task-success discordance $b/c$ with $\Phi $ and exact McNemar $p$. MC: means over 1000 regenerated sets. Synthetic $p$-values and intervals are conditional on the simulation assumptions; they are not empirical tests of the agent}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SV}{\ignorespaces Study~D, memory and retrieval. Upper block ACTUAL (real store, synthetic records); lower block \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)}. P(misleading): probability that the retrieved context misleads the planner; for the store it is set to the measured share of irrelevant top-1 records (DERIVED; that this share is the probability of misleading the planner is ASSUMED); for the filter it is an assumed input, so any effect of the filter follows from that assumption. With 44 repeated workflows the study cannot detect an effect of the assumed size (gain 0.08, loss 0.10). $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented.}}{4}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-memory}{{SV}{4}{Study~D, memory and retrieval. Upper block ACTUAL (real store, synthetic records); lower block \textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)}. P(misleading): probability that the retrieved context misleads the planner; for the store it is set to the measured share of irrelevant top-1 records (DERIVED; that this share is the probability of misleading the planner is ASSUMED); for the filter it is an assumed input, so any effect of the filter follows from that assumption. With 44 repeated workflows the study cannot detect an effect of the assumed size (gain 0.08, loss 0.10). $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {SIII-E}}Verification Overhead (Study F; H5)}{4}{subsection.3.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SVI}{\ignorespaces \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~E: 120 failure scenarios, 20 per mode; 27 drawn as permanent (25\% expected), none of them a missing confirmation. Entry paths and strategy sequences follow the code for the first failure of a task (each scenario has one failure; in the code later failures find the shared retry counter partly used): action errors, including a planner's request for help, are retried three times inline before the engine is called; a failed initial navigation is retried three times by the browser executor and then passed to the engine; a missing confirmation raises no error and is handled only by rejection and continuation. $^{\S  }$The retries before the engine remain. Per-attempt success rates are ASSUMED (PROPOSED for the guided variant), so differences between rows follow from them. Detected: failure noticed; FC: undetected missing confirmations reported complete; Unnec.: attempts on permanent failures; Att.: mean attempts. $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented.}}{5}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-recovery}{{SVI}{5}{\textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~E: 120 failure scenarios, 20 per mode; 27 drawn as permanent (25\% expected), none of them a missing confirmation. Entry paths and strategy sequences follow the code for the first failure of a task (each scenario has one failure; in the code later failures find the shared retry counter partly used): action errors, including a planner's request for help, are retried three times inline before the engine is called; a failed initial navigation is retried three times by the browser executor and then passed to the engine; a missing confirmation raises no error and is handled only by rejection and continuation. $^{\S }$The retries before the engine remain. Per-attempt success rates are ASSUMED (PROPOSED for the guided variant), so differences between rows follow from them. Detected: failure noticed; FC: undetected missing confirmations reported complete; Unnec.: attempts on permanent failures; Att.: mean attempts. $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SVII}{\ignorespaces Study~F, verification overhead. Upper block ACTUAL; p95 is the order statistic of rank $\mathrm  {round}(0.95(n-1))+1$. $^{*}$83 decisions on the constructed gate states, each the median of 20 repetitions (Section~\ref {sec:res-overhead}), measured on 2026-10-04 on a 2.1\,GHz machine; the other rows on 2026-10-23 on a 2.8\,GHz machine, so the two $\Phi $ rows are not directly comparable. Lower block \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)}, built from the ACTUAL component samples and an ASSUMED inference time, with re-planning after rejections and recovery attempts included; the two blocks are not combined into one claim. Inference takes 80\% of synthetic episode time under $\Phi $, evidence screenshots 4.1\%. $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented.}}{5}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-overhead}{{SVII}{5}{Study~F, verification overhead. Upper block ACTUAL; p95 is the order statistic of rank $\mathrm {round}(0.95(n-1))+1$. $^{*}$83 decisions on the constructed gate states, each the median of 20 repetitions (Section~\ref {sec:res-overhead}), measured on 2026-10-04 on a 2.1\,GHz machine; the other rows on 2026-10-23 on a 2.8\,GHz machine, so the two $\Phi $ rows are not directly comparable. Lower block \textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)}, built from the ACTUAL component samples and an ASSUMED inference time, with re-planning after rejections and recovery attempts included; the two blocks are not combined into one claim. Inference takes 80\% of synthetic episode time under $\Phi $, evidence screenshots 4.1\%. $^{\ddagger }$Not implemented}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {SVIII}{\ignorespaces \textsc  {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~H: robustness of the evidence-gated configuration (means over 300 regenerated sets per row, drawn from a random stream separate from the 1{,}000-set Monte Carlo of Table~\ref {tab:sim-tests}, so the unperturbed rows differ slightly from it). Without verification FCR stays at 0.36 at every continuation rate.}}{5}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sim-sens}{{SVIII}{5}{\textsc {Simulation-based (synthetic)} Study~H: robustness of the evidence-gated configuration (means over 300 regenerated sets per row, drawn from a random stream separate from the 1{,}000-set Monte Carlo of Table~\ref {tab:sim-tests}, so the unperturbed rows differ slightly from it). Without verification FCR stays at 0.36 at every continuation rate}{table.8}{}}
